Fixes the quoting rule from #1458 (part of it; see below). A formula into a sheet named `.x` was saved as `<f>.x!A1</f>`, and Excel 365 refuses to open that file, even with repair. Sheets named like an R1C1 reference (`R`, `RC`, `R1C1`, `r2c3`) also lost their quotes. A PivotTable from a sheet named `A1` reported its source as `A1!$A$1:$B$3`, and `ADDRESS(1,1,1,TRUE,"A1")` gave `A1!$A$1`.

GridCraft had three quoting rules: the formula printer, the engine's PivotTable code (also used by Solver and GETPIVOTDATA text), and ADDRESS. None of them matched Excel. There is now one rule in `gridcraft_core::addr` (`sheet_needs_quotes`, `quote_sheet`), and all three use it. The rule follows the measurements in #1458. A name is quoted when it:
- starts with a digit or `.`;
- contains anything other than letters (in any script), digits, `_` and `.`;
- reads as an A1 cell within the sheet (`XFD1` is quoted, `XFE1` is not);
- reads as an R1C1 reference: an optional `R` and digits, then an optional `C` and digits, in any case;
- is `TRUE` or `FALSE`. ADDRESS keeps Excel's exception and leaves these bare.

`gridcraft_formula::quote_sheet` and `printer::needs_quotes` stay as re-exports, so other callers don't change. #829 also edits `needs_quotes` (for external `[Book]Sheet` prefixes); after this PR its wrapper would call the core function.

This PR does **not** change the places that take quotes off again (`trim_matches('\'')` and the `strip_prefix` copies), the Name Box's handling of `''`, or Go To's hand-built prefix. Those are separate items in #1458 and are left for a follow-up, so this does not close the issue.

